42
Geographic Distribution
As in the previous censuses, Brunei Muara District remained the most populated district with
318,530 persons, comprising 72.3 per cent of the total population. This was followed by Belait
District (14.9 per cent or 65,531 persons), Tutong District (10.7 per cent or 47,210 pe rsons)
and Temburong District (2.1 per cent or 9,444 persons).
Age Distribution
From the age distribution aspect, about 20.5 per cent (90,376 persons) were below 15 years
of age, 73.2 per cent (322,445 persons) were in the working age group of 15-64 years and 6.3
per cent (27,894 persons) were aged 65 years and over.  The median age in 2021 was 3 1.7
years compared to 27.6 years in 2011.
Race
Malays with 67.4 per cent (297,016 persons) of the total population formed the largest
community in the country in 2021. The racial groups of Chinese and others comprised 9.6 per
cent (42,132 persons) and 23.0 per cent (101,567 persons) of the population respectively.
Religion
The majority of the country’s population were Muslims comprising of 82.1 per cent (362,035
persons) in 2021. The remaining population consisted of 6.7 per cent (29,462 persons)
Christians, 6.3 per cent (27,745 persons) Buddhists and 4.9 per cent (21,473 persons) were
members of others faiths or having no religious beliefs.
Marital Composition
Among the population aged 15 years and above, more than half (55.4 per cent or 193,965
persons) were married, while 38.6 per cent (135,137 pers ons) were single. The remaining 2.6
per cent (8,997 persons) were divorced and 3.5 per cent (12,240 persons) were widows.
Country of Birth
More than three -quarter (79.2 per ce nt or 349,135 persons) of the total population in 2021
were born in Brunei Darussalam, including 6,699 Temporary Residents. The number of foreign-
born population was 91,580 persons, accounting for 20.8 per cent of the total population. Out
of this number, 18.6 per cent (17,067 persons) were Brunei Citizens and Permanent Residents
who were born abroad.

